15662: Instruction Set
15663: 
15664: 8.1.4 Computation Modes
15665: The architecture allows for the following types of implementations:
15666: ? 64-bit implementations, in which all registers except some special-purpose registers (SPRs) are 64 bits long and effective addresses are 64 bits long. All 64-bit implementations have two modes of operation: 64-bit mode (which is the default) and 32-bit mode. The mode controls how the effective address is interpreted, how condition bits are set, and how the count register (CTR) is tested by branch conditional instructions. All instructions provided for 64-bit implementations are available in both 64- and 32-bit modes.
15667: ? 32-bit implementations, in which all registers except the FPRs are 32 bits long and effective addresses are 32 bits long.
15668: Note that the all pseudocode examples provided in this chapter are for 32-bit implementations. For more information on 64-bit and 32-bit modes, refer to Section 1.1.1, "The 64-Bit Architecture and the 32-Bit Subset."
15669: 8.2 Instruction Set
15670: 
25855: 30 31
25856: 
25857: n  SH r  ROTL(rS, n) m  MASK(MB, ME) rA  (r & m) | (rA & ? m)
25858: 
25859: The contents of rS are rotated left the number of bits specified by operand SH. A mask is generated having 1 bits from bit MB through bit ME and 0 bits elsewhere. The rotated data is inserted into rA under control of the generated mask.
25860: 
25861: Note that rlwimi can be used to insert a bit field into the contents of rA using the methods shown below:
25862: ? To insert an n-bit field, that is left-justified rS, into rA starting at bit position b, set SH = 32 ? b, MB = b, and ME = (b + n) ? 1.
25863: ? To insert an n-bit field, that is right-justified in rS, into rA starting at bit position b, set SH = 32 ? (b + n), MB = b, and ME = (b + n) ? 1.
25864: 
25932: 30 31
25933: 
25934: n  SH r  ROTL(rS, n) m  MASK(MB, ME) rA  r & m
25935: 
25936: The rS[0?31] contents are rotated left the number of bits specified by SH. A mask formed with 1 bits from MB through ME and 0 bits elsewhere is ANDed with the rotated data and the result is placed into rA. rlwinm can extract, rotate, shift, and clear bit fields as follows:
25937: 
25938: ? To extract an n-bit field that starts at bit position b in rS, right-justified into rA (clearing the remaining 32 ? n bits of rA), set SH = b + n, MB = 32 ? n, and ME = 31.
25939: ? To extract an n-bit field that starts at bit position b in rS, left-justified into rA (clearing the remaining 32 ? n bits of rA), set SH = b, MB = 0, and ME = n ? 1.
25940: ? To rotate register contents left or right by n bits, set SH = n (32 ? n), MB = 0, and ME = 31.
25941: ? To shift register contents right by n bits, set SH = 32 ? n, MB = n, and ME = 31. To clear the high-order b bits of a register and shift the result left by n bits, set SH = n, MB = b ? n and ME = 31 ? n.
25942: ? To clear a register's low-order n bits , set SH = 0, MB = 0, and ME = 31 ? n.
25943: 
